{"name":"New Frond","hex":"#bacca0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"new","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Newar."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Recently made, or created.","Of recent origin; having taken place recently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/new"},{"term":"frond","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The leaf of a fern, especially a compound leaf.","Any fern-like leaf or other object resembling a fern leaf."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frond"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frond","blocks":[{"text":"A frond is a large, divided leaf. In both common usage and botanical nomenclature, the leaves of ferns are referred to as fronds and some botanists restrict the term to this group. Other botanists allow the term frond to also apply to the large leaves of cycads, as well as palms (Arecaceae) and various other flowering plants, such as mimosa or sumac. \"Frond\" is commonly used to identify a large, compound leaf, but if the term is used botanically to refer to the leaves of ferns and algae it may be applied to smaller and undivided leaves. Whatever the term frond can also refer to structures or organisms of ediacaran lifeforms called Frondomorphs, which may be related to primitive animals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Davalia_frond_parts.png/330px-Davalia_frond_parts.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frond","wikidata":"Q2333821"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Fern_frond_lobed.jpg/330px-Fern_frond_lobed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Fern_frond_lobed.jpg/330px-Fern_frond_lobed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fern (D